  "answer": "Password cracking typically involves several techniques aimed at gaining unauthorized access to accounts by guessing or recovering passwords. The main methods include:\n\n1. **Brute Force Attack**: This method involves systematically trying all possible combinations of passwords for a known user ID until access is granted. It is often effective but can be time-consuming, especially if the password is complex and the account lockout policy is strict [Source 1].\n\n2. **Dictionary Attack**: This technique uses a pre-defined list of potential passwords (a dictionary) and attempts to match them against the target password. It is generally faster than brute force since it leverages common passwords that users might choose [Source 2].\n\n3. **Rainbow Table Attack**: This method involves using precomputed tables of hash values for passwords, allowing attackers to quickly look up the hash of a password and find the corresponding plaintext password [Source 2].\n\n4. **Exploiting Password Policies**: Attackers may also analyze the password policies in place (such as required complexity with numbers and special characters) to optimize their cracking attempts [Source 1].\n\nOverall, password cracking can be performed manually or automatically, and attackers often start with simpler passwords, as many users tend to select them [Source 3].",
